in on the left and our results  show that the improved guide RNA  scaffold enhances CRISPR knockdown  efficiency for nearly every guide we've  tested  but as I mentioned before CRISPR i  technology has remained relatively  unchanged since its development in 2013  but does CRISPR I benefit from the same  type of improvements as CRISPR a does  with additional effector domains a  recent paper in Nature Methods  described a targeted approach to find  new CRISPR repressors that might benefit  knockdown so they identified mecp2 which  is a chromatin modifying gene involved  in Rhett syndrome and fused its  transcriptional repressor domain to D  Cassadine crab  although mecp2 is thought to perform  other cellular functions the authors  reported that it enhanced CRISPR eye for  art to repress target genes  we performed a fairly comprehensive  comparison of all the different  combinations of guide modifications  guide designs and these competing  effectors to figure out which one would  be best for our customers however our  evaluation did not identify any  instances or dozens of guides where the  mecp2 construct was superior over crafty  cast nine especially when combined with  the improved guide RNA scaffold and the  UCSF guide design  as additional improvements we used D  Cassadine with an in terminal craft  fusion that further improves knockdown  efficiency as supported by Stanley  cheese lab and have now incorporated a  Yuko element in the promoter stabilize  gene expression  but during this evaluation we analyzed  the gene expression profiles of stable  cell lines containing either crafty  Cassadine or D Cassadine crab mecp2  using single-cell RNA seek so with the  non targeting control guide only the  expression profile of crafty Cassadine  cells was found to be similar to the  parental cell line here possible that  the crab mecp2 compound effector has  some promiscuous activity on the genome  in a targeting independent manner that  results in an expression profile drift  from the parental cell line so in this T  sneak plot each dot here represents a  single cell in this analysis Dell's with  more closely related gene expression  clustered together and you can see that  the purple and the orange points cluster  together those are the crab and the  parental cell lines whereas the green  mecp2 points segregate without this kind  of analysis this consequence would  likely have gone unnoticed by us so it's  important for your experiments that your  modified helper cell line remains as  faithful to your parental cell line as  possible for all of these reasons we've  developed our CRISPR I system and  libraries based on crafty cast nine and  the improved guide designs and scaffolds  described by the labs at UCSF so we  remain confident that our combination of  CRISPR I components is the best knock  down system available  so why should you add CRISPR I and  CRISPR I screening to your toolbox  prefer I is complementary to existing  gain-of-function and loss of function  technologies  unlike RNA I CRISPR I doesn't compete  for any endogenous cellular machinery  by permanently inhibiting June function  at the level of transcription using  CRISPR I we can observe increased  knockdown efficiency over RNA I that has  to keep disrupting the translation of  continuously transcribed mRNAs  the analogy here is you have a boat  that's taking on water it's more  efficient to plug the holes rather than  continuously bailing water we can now  also target non-coding RNAs or  inhibition with much more reliable loss  of function than would be possible with  CRISPR nucleases and unlike CRISPR s9  which needs to cut the DNA and create  function breaking in del CRISPR I  increases the odds of disrupting gene  function by only requiring binding at  the complex to each copy of the locus  and although crisper I can yield up to  99% knockdown efficiency in complete  loss of function can reveal essential  genes that would otherwise be overlooked  in a knockout screen  you  those who want to explore more focused  corners of the genome we offer custom  and soon off-the-shelf sub libraries  that categorize the genome into  functional groupings like the druggable  genome and non-coding genes  so although crisper screening is pretty  straightforward  and should not be thought of as scary to  undertake there are a number of  considerations to take before you start  the screen screens take reagents and  they take manpower really tight you need  to understand the ins and outs of your  cell line as it pertains to karyotype  proliferation rates drug resistance and  transduction efficiency you need to  understand the effect of your drug on  your cells for example how long do you  need to screen with your drug to get a  good enrichment to our phenotype or more  simple example does a practical amount  of your drug kill yourself in a  practical amount of time  and you need to have clear expectations  for your phenotypic outcome is it cell  death or proliferation  can you reliably assay a functional  change in reporter fluorescence or  enzyme activity for example  next-gen sequencing requires expertise  in the use of specialized equipment or  access to it and in bioinformatics  support and you need to try to  anticipate how you'll go about  validating and following up on your top  hits these are all pretty standard  considerations but we just want you to  remember to include them in your  planning for a CRISPR screen  when it comes to next-gen sequencing to  collect the fruits of your labor you'll  want to consider the number of samples  and the size of your data set you want  to maintain enough coverage during your  screens so that you don't have  artificial dropout of guides you'll want  to start with a large enough population  of cells to transduce  because you'll be subsampling at each  step after you harvest your genomic DNA  or PCR amplification you'll also want to  take measures to preserve as much  diversity as possible and maintain  representation of guides this means  you'll be doing a lot of PCR reactions  to amplify from as many unique genomes  collected as possible  we recommend maintaining about a 300 to  500 X coverage per guide throughout your  screening process  so for a library subtool with about  20,000 dives that means you'll expect  about 10 million reads to achieve that  depth there are a number of NGS  platforms available so you'll need to  understand the capacity of the one you  plan on using for your analysis a single  my cp3 flow cell for example gives about  25 million reads meaning you'd be able  to fit two samples on this slow cell  maximize your experimental efficiency by  figuring out your mass before you start  your screen  finally we've been talking a lot about  using these large format screens to  narrow down a list of relevant target  gene candidates but to take a deeper  functional genomics dive we can now  combine CRISPR screening with single  cell analysis but we've partnered with  10x genomics to develop compatible  reagents for crisper and crisper AI that  allow for individual targets or custom  pools to explore the transcriptional  changes in single cells for more  in-depth phenotypic analysis the  strategy is perfectly suited for  following up on top hits from a whole  genome screen using small or focused  pools and we've been seeing a lot of  excitement in the field of this recently  and we're very excited about the  possibilities for further development  here  Bart NX compatible vectors contain one  of four different capture sequences here  on the left the facilitate guide  identification in each cell on the right  you can see knocked out efficiency  across the different capture sequences  or one of our recommended positive  control guys  because we've observed differences in  capture efficiency for each capture  sequence between different cell lines  we've created an optimization kit with  controls containing each of the capture  sequences allowing you to determine  which is the best capture strategy for  your cell line in your experiment we  recommend starting with that  you  but before we end I just want to take a  brief step back to knockout screening  while pooled screening has been  predominantly performed using mortalized  cancer cell lines there's a growing need  for functional genomic platforms that  are amenable to passage limited primary  cells  traditionally cast nine or D cast nine  expressing zel lines are established  prior to the delivery of the guide pool  at stable cells primary cells that can't  be cultured for longer periods of time  just don't have this kind of time a  recent paper though detailed a  successful screen using a two-step  delivery strategy that allows for  transient nucleus of casting proteins  following linty guide pool delivery that  revealed key regulators of immune  function in primary human t-cells  and we recently deposited a paper in bio  archive that shows high functionality of  CRISPR nutcases  to drive gene knockouts and primary  t-cells as well with greater specificity  than calf nine so we're very excited  about the prospects of banding the power  of CRISPR and crisper screening for drug  discoveries  now using primary human t-cells and you  can see more about this in our portfolio  online  so I hope you come away from this talk  excited to explore CRISPR and  specifically CRISPR transcriptional  regulators and screening tools to  enhance your research pipeline and if  you have any questions that come up  later please feel free to email me and  check out our expanding portfolio at  sigma-aldrich comm slash CRISPR and  thanks for listening  you
 

